## [ISSUE] Same NPE remains in the paginated getSubGroupsStream overloads

**Severity:** MEDIUM - concurrent delete + list-children still returns HTTP 500

### The Problem
This PR guards against `modelSupplier.get()` returning `null` in `getSubGroupsCount()`. The three sibling methods a few lines above use the same pattern with no guard:

```java
// GroupAdapter.java:256, 262, 268
return modelSupplier.get().getSubGroupsStream(search, firstResult, maxResults);
return modelSupplier.get().getSubGroupsStream(firstResult, maxResults);
return modelSupplier.get().getSubGroupsStream(search, exact, firstResult, maxResults);
```

### Theory / Proof
`modelSupplier` is a `LazyModel` over `getGroupModel()` (`GroupAdapter.java:298-300`), which calls `cacheSession.getGroupDelegate().getGroupById(...)`. That returns `null` once another transaction has deleted the row. This is the root cause of #40368. It does not depend on which method makes the call.

`GroupResource.getSubGroups` (`services/.../admin/GroupResource.java:~181`) calls `group.getSubGroupsStream(search, exact, -1, -1)` on a `GroupAdapter` taken from the cache. If the group is deleted after the cache lookup and before this call, `modelSupplier.get()` is `null` and the call throws an NPE (HTTP 500). The admin UI uses the same endpoint to expand a group in the tree.

Other paths already handle a missing source. `CachedGroup`'s `DefaultLazyLoader` falls back to an empty value when the source is `null` (`DefaultLazyLoader.java:52`), and the JPA adapter filters out concurrently deleted groups (`jpa/GroupAdapter.java`, comment "In concurrent tests, the group might be deleted in another thread"). The paginated overloads are the only ones left unguarded.

### What happens today vs. what will break
The new test only exercises `GET /groups` (top-level listing plus `populateSubGroupCount`), so it passes. Running the same test against `GET /groups/{parentId}/children` while subgroups or the parent are deleted still reproduces the NPE.

### Research
The existing Keycloak cache adapters return an empty value when the delegate is `null`: `DefaultLazyLoader` falls back to `fallback.get()`, and `CachedGroup.getRoleMappings` returns `Collections.emptySet()`.

### Recommendation
Handle all four delegating methods the same way:

```java
private <T> Stream<T> fromModel(Function<GroupModel, Stream<T>> f) {
    GroupModel model = modelSupplier.get();
    return model == null ? Stream.empty() : f.apply(model);
}

@Override
public Stream<GroupModel> getSubGroupsStream(String search, Boolean exact, Integer firstResult, Integer maxResults) {
    if (isUpdated()) return updated.getSubGroupsStream(search, exact, firstResult, maxResults);
    return fromModel(m -> m.getSubGroupsStream(search, exact, firstResult, maxResults));
}
// same for the other two overloads
```

## [ISSUE] The regression test can pass without exercising the race and can leak its reader thread

**Severity:** LOW - the regression test is unreliable; it does not break the product

### The Problem
```java
new Thread(() -> {
    while (!deletedAll.get()) {
        try { managedRealm.admin().groups().groups(null, 0, Integer.MAX_VALUE, true); }
        catch (Exception e) { caughtExceptions.add(e); }
    }
}).start();

groupUuuids.forEach(id -> managedRealm.admin().groups().group(id).remove());
deletedAll.set(true);
assertThat(caughtExceptions, Matchers.empty());
```

### Theory / Proof
1. **No join.** `assertThat` runs right after `deletedAll.set(true)`. A request that is still in flight at that point and then fails adds to `caughtExceptions` after the assertion has already passed. That last request is also the one most likely to see a deleted group.
2. **No proof of overlap.** Nothing records that the reader finished even one request while deletes were running. On a fast machine, 100 local deletes can finish before the new thread's first HTTP call returns, and the test passes without the fix. It is non-deterministic in the direction that hides regressions.
3. **Thread leak.** If any `remove()` throws, `deletedAll` is never set. The reader thread (non-daemon, unnamed) keeps sending `GET /groups` for the rest of the suite and can slow down or disturb later tests on the shared server.

### What happens today vs. what will break
With the fix in place the test is green either way, so these problems stay hidden. They matter when someone reverts or refactors the fix and the test still passes.

### Research
The usual JUnit pattern for concurrency tests is an `ExecutorService` or a joined thread, a `finally` that stops the worker, and an assertion that the reader actually ran (for example, a read counter greater than 0).

### Recommendation
```java
AtomicInteger reads = new AtomicInteger();
Thread reader = new Thread(() -> {
    while (!deletedAll.get()) {
        try {
            managedRealm.admin().groups().groups(null, 0, Integer.MAX_VALUE, true);
            reads.incrementAndGet();
        } catch (Exception e) {
            caughtExceptions.add(e);
        }
    }
}, "group-reader");
reader.setDaemon(true);
reader.start();
try {
    groupUuuids.forEach(id -> managedRealm.admin().groups().group(id).remove());
} finally {
    deletedAll.set(true);
    reader.join(TimeUnit.SECONDS.toMillis(30));
}
assertThat(reads.get(), Matchers.greaterThan(0));
assertThat(caughtExceptions, Matchers.empty());
```
